### 6. malloc returning a typed struct
`Node *node = malloc(sizeof *node);` (or `malloc(sizeof(Node))`). Model: when the result of `malloc` is assigned to a `StructName *` variable (or returned as one), allocate a heap STRUCT of that type with all fields default-initialized (scalars 0, pointers null). You can infer the struct type from the declared pointer type of the assignment target / the surrounding declaration. `sizeof <anything>` should just evaluate to a dummy positive integer (we don't model real bytes) — parse `sizeof expr` and `sizeof(type)` and return e.g. 1. Add `sizeof` as a keyword or handle it as a special call/operator.
- Because malloc needs to know the target struct type, a clean approach: in `exec_var_decl` for a pointer-to-struct initialized by a malloc call, allocate the struct of the declared type. Also handle `node = malloc(...)` assignments and `return malloc(...)` where the function returns a struct pointer. If inferring type is hard in all cases, at minimum make the common pattern `StructName *x = malloc(...)` and `x->field = ...` work.

### 7. NULL, exit, perror
- `NULL` is a null pointer literal (lex as identifier `NULL`, or handle in parser/interp as a null pointer). Comparisons `root == NULL` and `root != NULL` must work (a null pointer compares equal to NULL / to 0).
- `exit(code)` — stop execution cleanly (you can model by returning a special flow that unwinds all frames, or just stop adding states and finish the trace).
- `perror(str)` — print the string to stdout (append to the interpreter's stdout buffer), returns void.

### 8. Snapshotting struct state
In the `snapshot` function (interp.rs), when building the heap for the trace, emit heap struct instances as `HeapObject::Struct { id, type_name, fields }` where each field is a `trace::Variable { name, value, declared_type }`. A pointer field pointing at another heap struct becomes `Value::heap_ref(target_id)` so the frontend draws the node→node arrow (this is how the BST tree and linked list will render). A null pointer field is `Value::reference(None)`. Scalar fields are `Value::primitive(...)`. Keep existing array-heap emission working.

## Constraints & correctness
- DO NOT break existing functionality: scalars, int arrays, basic int pointers (&x, *p), loops, conditionals, functions, recursion, printf, division/modulo-by-zero errors, array out of bounds, null deref. There are existing tests in `backend/src/main.rs` (`runs_simple_loop_and_array`, `pointer_and_deref`, `reports_division_by_zero`, `classifies_index_out_of_bounds`, `classifies_null_deref`, `classifies_missing_semicolon`, `classifies_unexpected_char`) — ALL must still pass.
- Keep the trace JSON shape unchanged (only emit the already-existing `HeapObject::Struct` variant).
- Keep the existing per-statement + function-entry snapshot behavior and the `depth` field (used for step-over/into/out).
- Respect the existing step limit / recursion guards.

