Here's what I'd reach for, depending on what you want to exercise. There isn't one program that hits everything *and* stays readable, so I'll give you a tiered set — but if you want a single "kitchen sink," I'll point to it at the end.

**Best all-around demo — stdin + array + loop + the visualizer, no errors:**

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);

    int values[10];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &values[i]);
    }

    // Bubble sort — lots of state change to watch, per iteration.
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
            if (values[j] > values[j + 1]) {
                int tmp = values[j];
                values[j] = values[j + 1];
                values[j + 1] = tmp;
            }
        }
    }

    printf("sorted: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", values[i]);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```
**stdin:** `5 4 2 5 1 3`
This exercises `scanf("%d", &values[i])` (scanf writing into an *array cell* via `&arr[i]` — a good test of the pointer-write path), nested loops, and the array cells flashing/swapping as you step through — great for the step-through + change-highlight features. Output: `sorted: 1 2 3 4 5`.

**Best for the pointer/heap visualizer — the BST** (your earlier one), since it shows the horizontal tree layout, struct heap objects, and left/right arrows. Pairs stdin nicely:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

typedef struct Node { int value; struct Node *left; struct Node *right; } Node;

Node *insert(Node *root, int v) {
    if (root == NULL) {
        Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
        n->value = v; n->left = NULL; n->right = NULL;
        return n;
    }
    if (v < root->value) root->left = insert(root->left, v);
    else if (v > root->value) root->right = insert(root->right, v);
    return root;
}

void inorder(Node *root) {
    if (root == NULL) return;
    inorder(root->left);
    printf("%d ", root->value);
    inorder(root->right);
}

int main(void) {
    Node *root = NULL;
    int n;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        int v;
        scanf("%d", &v);
        root = insert(root, v);
    }
    inorder(root);
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```
**stdin:** `7 8 3 10 1 6 14 4`
This reads how many values, then builds the tree from stdin — so the tree differs per input, and you get the full tree layout + recursion stepping.

**Best for exercising the tutor / new error catching** — one small program per error kind:
- **arity mismatch:** `int add(int a,int b){return a+b;} int main(void){ return add(1); }` → tutor coaches `arg_count_mismatch`.
- **no such field:** a struct access to a field that doesn't exist → `no_such_field`.
- **the classics:** divide by zero, `arr[5]` on a length-3 array, `*p` on a `NULL` pointer.

**A single "kitchen sink"** if you want one program that touches stdin + loops + a struct + a pointer error you can then fix: a `while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1)` loop that reads an unknown count of numbers until EOF, pushes them into a linked list, and prints them back — it tests EOF-terminated scanf, `malloc` structs, and the linked-list arrow layout all at once.

My pick for a first run after these changes: **the bubble sort with stdin** — it's the cleanest showcase of the brand-new scanf-into-array-cell path plus the step-through visualization, and it's easy to eyeball for correctness (`sorted: 1 2 3 4 5`). Want me to spin up the servers so you can paste one of these in, or drop one into the editor's starter code?

